WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.782
In this lesson we will look at the notion of
continuity of a

2
00:00:03.782 --> 00:00:06.900
function, the classification of discontinuity
points, and

3
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:12.520
the common practical task of studying a
function for continuity.

4
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:19.500
The notion of continuity of a function, like
the notion of a limit,

5
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.620
is one of the basic notions of mathematical
analysis.

6
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:29.720
But what is continuity of a function?

7
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:33.112
It is easy to imagine a line that can be drawn
without

8
00:00:33.112 --> 00:00:36.190
lifting the pencil from the paper. If such a
line

9
00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:42.440
is drawn, it is continuous. This line is the
graph of a continuous function.

10
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:46.067
Graphically, a function is continuous at a
point

11
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:49.240
if its graph does not break at that point.

12
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:59.560
A function f(x) defined in a neighborhood of a
point x₀ is called continuous at x₀

13
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:06.220
if the limit of the function at that point
equals its value,

14
00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:13.060
that is, the limit of f(x) as x approaches x₀
equals the value

15
00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.580
of the function at that point.

16
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:22.360
If a function f(x) is defined in some
neighborhood of a point x₀

17
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:28.860
but is not continuous at x₀ itself, it is
called

18
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:35.140
discontinuous, and x₀ is called a point of
discontinuity.

19
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:39.679
Let us look at the notion of a continuous
function

20
00:01:39.679 --> 00:01:42.220
and points of discontinuity in a drawing.

21
00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:49.160
The picture shows schematic graphs of four
functions: two of them

22
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:55.440
are continuous at x = a, and two have a
discontinuity at x = a.

23
00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:03.830
Continuity can also be one-sided. Let us
explain what this means.

24
00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:10.780
Look at this drawing: if the right-hand limit
at the point x₀

25
00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:17.050
equals the value of the function, the function
is called right-continuous.

26
00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:23.590
If the left-hand limit at the point x₀ equals
the value of the function,

27
00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:28.090
the function is called left-continuous.

28
00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:34.780
All points of discontinuity are divided into
discontinuities

29
00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.100
of the first and second kind.

30
00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:44.140
If at this point f(x) has finite but unequal
left and right

31
00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:50.680
limits, the point x₀ is called a discontinuity
of the first kind.

32
00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:57.540
This definition does not require the function
to be defined at the point x₀ itself:

33
00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:04.540
it is enough that it is defined to the left
and right of it. The point x₀

34
00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:11.400
is called a discontinuity of the second kind
if at this point the function f(x)

35
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:18.320
lacks at least one of the one-sided limits, or
at least one of them

36
00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.180
is infinite.

37
00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.800
Let us look at an example.

38
00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.405
We need to study the function for continuity
and,

39
00:03:27.405 --> 00:03:31.120
if there are points of discontinuity,
determine their type.

40
00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:37.660
This function exists for all x, but it is made
up of three different

41
00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:40.235
functions, so it is not elementary.

42
00:03:40.235 --> 00:03:44.060
Let us study its behavior near the points x =
−1 and

43
00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:51.100
x = 1, where its analytic expression changes.

44
00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.900
First we find the values of the one-sided
limits

45
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.696
at the point minus one. The one-sided limits
are

46
00:03:57.696 --> 00:04:00.860
equal, the function exists at this point

47
00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:04.525
and equals three, so the function is
continuous

48
00:04:04.525 --> 00:04:07.800
at this point. Now let us study continuity

49
00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:14.640
at the point x = 1. The values of the
one-sided limits are finite and

50
00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:21.640
not equal to each other, so the point x = 1 is
a discontinuity of the first kind.

51
00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:25.850
Let us look at the graph of this function.

52
00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:29.705
Note that at this point the function jumps:

53
00:04:29.705 --> 00:04:33.470
this is a discontinuity of the first kind.

54
00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:43.960
Let us look at an example: the function f(x) =
1/x is given.

55
00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:47.523
We need to study this function for continuity,

56
00:04:47.523 --> 00:04:50.530
determine the type of its discontinuities

57
00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:52.700
and make a drawing.

58
00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:59.640
This function is continuous on the whole
number line except at the point x = 0.

59
00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:03.498
We study continuity following the standard
scheme.

60
00:05:03.498 --> 00:05:06.580
The function is not defined at this point

61
00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:13.350
because the denominator becomes zero. We
compute the one-sided limits. Recall that

62
00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.719
the notation minus zero means an infinitely

63
00:05:16.719 --> 00:05:20.010
small negative number, and plus zero means

64
00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:26.600
an infinitely small positive number. The
one-sided limits are infinite, so zero

65
00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:30.622
is a discontinuity of the second kind. The
graph of this function with

66
00:05:30.622 --> 00:05:34.300
its point of discontinuity can be drawn in the
coordinate plane.

67
00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:42.278
Let us sum up the lesson. Studying a function

68
00:05:42.278 --> 00:05:45.620
for continuity means answering the question

69
00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:49.052
of whether it has points of discontinuity and,

70
00:05:49.052 --> 00:05:52.260
if so, determining the nature of each point

71
00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:55.600
and assigning it to the corresponding class.

72
00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:02.200
Studying the continuity of a function at
points helps solve many problems.

73
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.773
To solve a whole range of problems,

74
00:06:04.773 --> 00:06:10.360
it is useful to master the skill of studying a
function for discontinuities.
